The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
if (!aa_unpack_u32(e, &pdb->start[0], "start"))
    pdb->start[0] = DFA_START;
if (!aa_unpack_u32(e, &pdb->start[AA_CLASS_FILE], "dfa_start"))
    pdb->start[AA_CLASS_FILE] = DFA_START;
```
```
pdb->start[i] = aa_dfa_next(pdb->dfa, pdb->start[0], i);
```
Reason:
The start states are read from untrusted policy data without verifying they are within the DFA state table bounds. When `aa_dfa_next` is later called with these values as indices (e.g., `pdb->start[0]` in the loop), an out-of-bounds read occurs if the start state exceeds the number of states in the DFA.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
size_t state_count = pdb->dfa->tables[YYTD_ID_BASE]->td_lolen;
if (pdb->start[0] >= state_count ||
    pdb->start[AA_CLASS_FILE] >= state_count) {
    *info = "invalid dfa start state";
    goto fail;
}
```
Reason:
The fix adds a bounds check after unpacking the start states. It retrieves the number of DFA states from the base table length and rejects the policy if either start state is out of bounds, preventing the subsequent out-of-bounds read in `aa_dfa_next`.

